BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T153721Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields. COMPENSATION: USD 155\,000 t
 o 285\,000 per year gross depending on location\, seniority and experience
 \, plus insurance\, retirement plan\, PTO (17 days US / 20 days UK)\, conf
 erence funding\, catered meals\, and up to USD 25\,000 in relocation suppo
 rt. ELIGIBILITY: at least one substantial piece of research-caliber work\;
  credentials are not required. Full-time (part-time case by case for acade
 mics on sabbatical)\, in person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or 
 Washington DC\, with visa support where required. TIMELINE: the Winter 202
 7 intake window runs 25 August to 31 October 2026 (AoE)\, with decisions b
 y end of November and interviews plus a paid work test in the first week o
 f December\; three intake windows per year.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Alig
 nment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berkele
 y\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. For 
 experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-ca
 liber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by locati
 on\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insuran
 ce\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meals\
 , and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuration:
  6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year.\
 n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tate-delfina-brooks-international-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T153721Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tate x Delfina Foundation: Brooks International Fellowshi
 p Programme 2027 (London)
DESCRIPTION:Now in its thirteenth year\, the Brooks International Fellowshi
 p Programme places international researchers within Tate Britain's Curator
 ial Division for six months\, with accommodation and a parallel residency 
 programme at Delfina Foundation. TWO FELLOWSHIPS IN 2027: Leonora Carringt
 on: Art and the Theatre of Life\, researching the British-Mexican artist's
  engagement with theatre and performance (knowledge of Mexican social-poli
 tical context and performance history particularly useful)\; and Artist Su
 pported Studios\, research and development for the 2028 Tate Britain Commi
 ssion focused on supported studios working with disabled and neurodivergen
 t artists. WHAT YOU GET: return economy flight\, UK airport transfers\, vi
 sa fee reimbursement and visa support\, GBP 40 per day living allowance\, 
 London transport pass\, a single bedroom at Delfina Foundation\, travel to
  other Tate sites and UK/European institutions\, plus Delfina's programme 
 of visits\, presentations and professional development\; the Carrington fe
 llowship adds a research resource allowance. ELIGIBILITY: international re
 searchers based outside the UK. APPLICATION: not online\; download the Wor
 d application form for your chosen fellowship from Tate's job portal and e
 mail it to international.fellowships@tate.org.uk (alternative formats such
  as video or audio are described in the Open Call PDF). DEADLINE: 21 Octob
 er 2026\, 17:00 GMT\; late applications not accepted.\n\nOrganisation: Tat
 e and Delfina Foundation\nLocation: Tate Britain Curatorial Division\, Lon
 don\, UK\, with accommodation and complementary activities at Delfina Foun
 dation. Open to international researchers based outside the UK.\nAward: No
 n-salaried but fully supported: return economy-class flight\, UK airport t
 ransfers\, visa fee reimbursement and visa support via Delfina Foundation\
 , a living allowance bursary of GBP 40 per day\, a London Underground and 
 bus travel pass\, a single bedroom at Delfina Foundation with shared facil
 ities\, and travel to other Tate galleries and UK/European cultural instit
 utions. The Leonora Carrington fellowship adds a research resource allowan
 ce. No application fee.\nDuration: Six months\, 5 April to 17 September 20
 27.\n\nMore: https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-cal
 l-with-tate-for-brooks-international-fellowship-programme-2027/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-with-tat
 e-for-brooks-international-fellowship-programme-2027/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tate Britain Curatorial Division\, London\, UK\, with accommodatio
 n and complementary activities at Delfina Foundation. Open to internationa
 l researchers based outside the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cove-park-awarded-residencies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T153721Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261211
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261212
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cove Park Awarded Residencies 2027-2028 (Scotland\, UK\, 
 Central and South America and diasporas)
DESCRIPTION:Cove Park's biennial call for fully funded Awarded Residencies 
 at its 50-acre rural residency site in Argyll\, on the west coast of Scotl
 and\, programmed between May 2027 and December 2028. Residencies run from 
 2 weeks up to 12 weeks (average four weeks) and can be split across the tw
 o years around caring or work commitments\; international residents can co
 unt slow-travel time as part of the funded period. Open to artists\, creat
 ive practitioners\, writers\, translators and researchers in all art forms
  and at every career stage\, including collectives and collaborative group
 s\; no formal qualification needed. Priorities include work responding to 
 climate breakdown\, sound artists and musicians (new Sound Studio)\, and w
 ork on home\, place\, language (Scots\, Gaelic\, BSL)\, displacement and b
 elonging. No final presentation is required. ELIGIBILITY: based in the UK\
 , Central America or South America\; Central and South American diaspora a
 rtists based in Europe or North America\; or born in Scotland and living e
 lsewhere. Anyone in full- or part-time formal education on 11 December 202
 6 is not eligible. SUPPORT: GBP 775 per week fee\; travel allowance (Scotl
 and GBP 65 to 100 Central Belt\, up to GBP 200 Borders/Highlands/Islands\;
  rest of UK up to GBP 250\; Europe up to GBP 400\; other international up 
 to GBP 1\,250)\; materials up to GBP 150\; visa support\; private self-cat
 ering accommodation (suitable for collaborators and those attending with c
 hildren) and private studios. An Application Access Bursary (e.g. to pay a
  support worker) is available for applicants facing barriers due to disabi
 lity or neurodivergence\; request it from Alex Marrs by 30 November 2026. 
 Video or audio applications accepted. Applicants are asked not to use gene
 rative AI in their application. APPLY via the online form: 500-word statem
 ent\, 100-word bio\, CV\, up to 8 images or video/audio links or writing s
 amples. SELECTION: shortlisting by the programme team and former residents
 \; shortlist notified by 12 February 2027\; Zoom interviews the week of 22
  February 2027. Contact: Alex Marrs\, alex@covepark.org. DEADLINE: Friday 
 11 December 2026\, 5pm GMT.\n\nOrganisation: Cove Park\nLocation: Cove Par
 k\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, private self-cat
 ering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in the UK\, Central 
 or South America\, Central and South American diaspora artists based in Eu
 rope or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living anywhere. Student
 s in formal education are not eligible.\nAward: Fully funded: GBP 775 per 
 week fee (VAT inclusive\, freelance)\, travel allowance (Scotland GBP 65 t
 o 200\, rest of UK up to GBP 250\, Europe up to GBP 400\, other internatio
 nal up to GBP 1\,250)\, materials up to GBP 150 for studio-based practitio
 ners\, private accommodation and studio\, visa and access support. Applica
 tion Access Bursary available (request by 30 November 2026). No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: 2 to 12 weeks (average 4)\, between May 2027 and Decembe
 r 2028\n\nMore: https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cove Park\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, 
 private self-catering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in t
 he UK\, Central or South America\, Central and South American diaspora art
 ists based in Europe or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living a
 nywhere. Students in formal education are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eastside-projects-radical-sabbatical-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T153721Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261120
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261121
SUMMARY:Deadline: Radical Sabbatical 2027: six-month residencies on touch a
 t the University of Birmingham (GBP 3\,500 fee + GBP 500 production\, UK-b
 ased artists)
DESCRIPTION:Radical Sabbatical is a collaboration between Eastside Projects
  and University Collections at the University of Birmingham that gives two
  artists a six-month residency centred on research and development. The 20
 27 edition is themed around touch: artists are matched with one or more ac
 ademics and asked to interrogate and interpret academic research\, with ar
 eas including digital touch and technology\, haptic and sensory experience
 \, embodied knowledge\, disability and inclusive design\, medical perspect
 ives\, materiality and craft\, memory and emotion\, and human and non-huma
 n sensory connection. Academic partners include the Active Touch Lab and t
 he Department of Creative Industries. Artists have regular one-to-one conv
 ersations with staff and present at a final sharing event. ELIGIBILITY: ar
 tists based in the UK. SUPPORT: GBP 3\,500 artist fee plus GBP 500 product
 ion budget. TIMELINE: deadline Friday 20 November 2026\, 11:59pm\; intervi
 ews for shortlisted artists 10 December 2026\; residencies begin January 2
 027\; final sharing event and evaluation June/July 2027. APPLY using the a
 rtists' callout pack linked from the call page\; questions to info@eastsid
 eprojects.org.\n\nOrganisation: Eastside Projects with University Collecti
 ons\, University of Birmingham\nLocation: University of Birmingham\, Birmi
 ngham\, UK (in person\, research residency). Open to artists based in the 
 UK only.\nAward: GBP 3\,500 artist fee plus GBP 500 production budget per 
 artist\; two artists selected.\nDuration: 6 months\, January to June/July 
 2027\n\nMore: https://eastsideprojects.org/projects/apply-now-radical-sabb
 atical-2027-residencies/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=
 referral
URL:https://eastsideprojects.org/projects/apply-now-radical-sabbatical-2027
 -residencies/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:University of Birmingham\, Birmingham\, UK (in person\, research r
 esidency). Open to artists based in the UK only.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
